          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#adiabatic_switching Adiabatic switching
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %em adiabatic switching
            refers to considering a smooth transition between vanishing and non-vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic"} adiabatically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
            which are quantities that give well defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
            (hence
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
          %p
            Originally adiabatic switching was considered (
            %a{:href => "#LippmannSchwinger50"}> Lippmann-Schwinger 50
            ) only in the
            = succeed "-direction" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(- \epsilon {\Vert t \Vert})
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \in (0,\infty)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\Vert t\Vert}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            of the time
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Strocchi13"} Strocchi 13, section 6.3
          %p
            Using this, the
            %em Gell-Mann and Low formula
            (
            %a{:href => "#GellMannLow51"}> Gell-Mann &amp; Low 51
            , see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Molinari06"} Molinari 06
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenstates"} eigenstates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \psi \rangle
            of an interacting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi free
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi int
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = H_{free} + H_{int}
            in terms of the eigenstates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi free
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \Psi_{free} \rangle
            of the free Hamiltonian by the “adiabatic limit”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi int
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∝
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ±
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi free
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vert \Psi^{\pm}_{int} \rangle
                  \\;\propto\;
                  \\underset{\epsilon \to 0}{\lim}
                  S_\epsilon(0, \pm \infty)
                  \\vert \Psi_{free} \rangle
          %p
            (if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            exists) where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\epsilon
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            of the adiabatically switched Hamiltonian
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi ϵ
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi free
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi int
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_\epsilon \coloneqq H_{free} + e^{- \epsilon {\Vert t\Vert}}H_{int}
          %p
            More generally, one may consider adiabatic switching taking place not just in time, but in all of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            In the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via the method of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi int
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{int}
            used in the mathematical construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            are multiplied with a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"}> coupling constant
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            which is in fact taken to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi free
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi int
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_g = L_{free} + g L_{int}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            as modeled by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi T
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_g \coloneqq T \exp( \tfrac{i}{\hbar} \int_{X} g :L_{int}(x): )
          %p
            is non-trivial only on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            Since the actual interactions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            are of course
            %em not
            “switched off” anywhere, the use of an adiabatic switching is just an intermediate mathematical step. Originally in (
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
            ) the idea was that after having constructed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for any adiabatic switching
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            (“adiabatic limit”)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \to 1
            had to be taken to remove the switching in the end. Failure of this limit to exist is interpreted as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergency"}> infrared divergency
            ” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (since the divergency comes from large scales, hence long
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavelength"} wavelength
          %p
            But as observed in (
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"}> Il’in-Slavnov 78
            ) and rediscovered in (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            ), an adiabatic switching map that is unity on a globally hyperbolic sub-spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O \subset X
            is sufficient to compute the perturbative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra"} interacting field algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} algebra of quantum observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(O)
            on that subspace, and the collection of all of these as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            ranges forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+net+of+observables"} causally local net of observables
            which fully captures the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in the sense of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}> this prop.
            ). This perspective is now known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant algebraic quantum field theory
          %h3#adiabatic_limit Adiabatic limit
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative S-matrix
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
            is removed (if it exists) is called the
            %em adiabatic limit
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em strong adiabatic limit
          %p
            If one just asks that the corresponding limit exists for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            one speaks of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em weak adiabatic limit
          %p
            Even with the adiabatically switched S-matrix elements (not taking a limit) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+quantum+observables"} local net of quantum observables
            is well defined (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}> this prop.
            ), this is hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{O} \mapsto \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})
          %p
            that assigns
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+closed+subsets"} causally closed subsets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi 𝒪
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{A} \coloneqq \underset{\underset{\mathcal{O}}{\longrightarrow}}{\lim} \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})
          %p
            over this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            (in the sense of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em algebraic adiabatic limit
          %p
            (See around
            %a{:href => "#Duch17"} Duch 17, section 4
            for review of strong, weak and algebraic adiabaitc limit; and
            %a{:href => "#Duch17"} Duch 17, chapter II
            for results on the weak adiabatic limit)
          %p Here
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em algebraic adiabatic
                limit defines the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                in the limit;
            %li
              %p
                the weak adiabatic limit may serve to define also the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+a+star-algebra"} states
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+vacuum"} interacting vacuum
                (
                %a{:href => "#Duch17"}> Duch 17, p. 113-114
                ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic"} adiabatic
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept of adiabatic switching in the time direction was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#LippmannSchwinger50
              B. A. Lippmann,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julian+Schwinger"} Julian Schwinger
              = succeed "," do
                %em Variational Principles for Scattering Processes. I
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.79.469"} doi:10.1103/PhysRev.79.469
          %p reviewed for instance in
          %ul
            %li#Strocchi13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franco+Strocchi"}> Franco Strocchi
              , section 6.3 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Introduction to Non-Perturbative Foundations of Quantum Field Theory
          %p and the corresponding formula for the interacting eigenstates in terms of the free ones is due to
          %ul
            %li#GellMannLow51
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Murray+Gell-Mann"}> Murray Gell-Mann
                , F. Low,
                %em Bound states in quantum field theory
                Phys. Rev. 84, 350 (1951)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Murray+Gell-Mann"}> Murray Gell-Mann
                , M. L. Goldberger, Phys. Rev. 91 398 (1953)
          %p see
          %ul
            %li#Molinari06
              Luca Guido Molinari,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Another proof of Gell-Mann and Low’s theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0612030"} arXiv:math-ph/0612030
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                C. Lanczos, R. C. Clark, G. H. Derrick (eds.), Ch. 2, Sec 2 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Methods in Solid State and Superfluid Theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4899-6435-9"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4899-6435-9
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gell-Mann_and_Low_theorem"} Gell-Mann and Low theorem
          %p
            The generalization to switching in all space-time directions was considered for the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#EpsteinGlaser73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"} Henri Epstein
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Glaser"} Vladimir Glaser
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Role+of+locality+in+perturbation+theory"} The Role of locality in perturbation theory
          %p
            The observation that this in fact makes causal perturbation theory a tool for constructing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#IlinSlavnov78
              %p
                V. A. Il’in and D. S. Slavnov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} math-ph/9903028
          %p
            The term “algebraic adiabatic limit” for the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            (or its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+limit"} inductive limit
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenLindner13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Falk+Lindner"} Falk Lindner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Construction of KMS States in Perturbative QFT and Renormalized Hamiltonian Dynamics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.6519"} arXiv:1306.6519
          %p
            The weak adiabatic limit in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            for massive fields was shown to exists in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
          %p
            Extension of this result to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            was given in
          %ul
            %li#BlanchardSeneor75
              P. Blanchard and R. Seneor,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Green’s functions for theories with massless particles (in perturbation theory)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=AIHPA_1975__23_2_147_0"} Numdam
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
              , section 3.11 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
          %p Further extension of the result is due to
          %ul
            %li#Duch17
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pawe%C5%82+Duch"}> Paweł Duch
              ,
              %em Massless fields and adiabatic limit in quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.09907"}> arXiv:1709.09907
              )
        .revisedby
